        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009178</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>diamide</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009178"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Diamide is primarily used as an insecticide with evidence of mammalian toxicity. This compound is chemically classified as being part of the monoazo group. FungAMR listed this compound as having some anti-fungal properties.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:21015756</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009179 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009179">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009323"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Diethofencarb is a fungicide typically used as an agrochemical. It is a carbamate ester class fungicide, an aromatic ether and a carbanilate fungicide (subclass of carbamate fungicide). Carbamates are functionally related to benzimidazoles.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>CAS:87130-20-9</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>CHEBI:4520</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>ChEMBL:CHEMBL1880404</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PubChem:91742</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009179</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>diethofencarb</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009179"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Diethofencarb is a fungicide typically used as an agrochemical. It is a carbamate ester class fungicide, an aromatic ether and a carbanilate fungicide (subclass of carbamate fungicide). Carbamates are functionally related to benzimidazoles.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:1388107</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009180 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009180">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3007500"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An agricultural fungicide that is a systemic conazole (imidazole) fungicide used for the control of many seed-borne and soil-borne diseases and fungi particularly Ascomycetes and Basidiomycetes. It has a broad spectrum of action, being systemic with protective and curative action, but is known to disrupt membrane function. It also has a role as a chelator. It is a tertiary alcohol, a member of triazoles and a dichlorobenzene. In FungAMR, it was listed as having effects against Aspergillus fumigatus. It is commonly used in the control of apple, coffee and peanut diseases, but is also used for various other fruits and vegetables. Major consumption is in Asian countries and it is used mainly for the control of rice sheath blight in China, India, Vietnam, and parts of East Asia. It was introduced in the 1980s, it is not approved for use within the European Union.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>CAS:79983-71-4</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>CHEBI:83711</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>ChEMBL:CHEMBL1898860</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PubChem:66461</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009180</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>hexaconazole</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009180"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>An agricultural fungicide that is a systemic conazole (imidazole) fungicide used for the control of many seed-borne and soil-borne diseases and fungi particularly Ascomycetes and Basidiomycetes. It has a broad spectrum of action, being systemic with protective and curative action, but is known to disrupt membrane function. It also has a role as a chelator. It is a tertiary alcohol, a member of triazoles and a dichlorobenzene. In FungAMR, it was listed as having effects against Aspergillus fumigatus. It is commonly used in the control of apple, coffee and peanut diseases, but is also used for various other fruits and vegetables. Major consumption is in Asian countries and it is used mainly for the control of rice sheath blight in China, India, Vietnam, and parts of East Asia. It was introduced in the 1980s, it is not approved for use within the European Union.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:24084639</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009181 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009181">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3007499"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An agricultural fungicide that is a member of the triazole antifungal class.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>CAS:107534-96-3</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>CHEBI:83779</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>ChEMBL:CHEMBL487186</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PubChem:86102</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009181</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>tebuconazole</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009181"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>An agricultural fungicide that is a member of the triazole antifungal class.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:32186508</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009182 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009182">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3007499"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A fungicide that is used commercially for agriculture. It is also used in combination with permethrin in formulations of wood preserver. The compound is known as a DMI, or demethylation inhibiting fungicide, due to its binding with and inhibition of the 14-alpha demethylase enzyme from demethylating a precursor to ergosterol. Without this demethylation step, the ergosterols are not incorporated into the growing fungal cell membranes, and cellular growth is stopped.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>CAS:60207-90-1</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>CHEBI:8489</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>ChEMBL:CHEMBL560579</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PubChem:43234</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009182</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>propiconazole</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009182"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A fungicide that is used commercially for agriculture. It is also used in combination with permethrin in formulations of wood preserver. The compound is known as a DMI, or demethylation inhibiting fungicide, due to its binding with and inhibition of the 14-alpha demethylase enzyme from demethylating a precursor to ergosterol. Without this demethylation step, the ergosterols are not incorporated into the growing fungal cell membranes, and cellular growth is stopped.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:29507067</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009183 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009183">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009165"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A fungicide used for the control of rice blast, it is not approved for use within the European Union. It has a role as a melanin synthesis inhibitor and an antifungal agrochemical. According to CHEBI, this compound is not a member of the azole group of antifungals, but is instead part of a group called the triazobenzothiazoles.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>CAS:41814-78-2</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>CHEBI:81783</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>ChEMBL:CHEMBL357021</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PubChem:39040</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009183</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>tricyclazole</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009183"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A fungicide used for the control of rice blast, it is not approved for use within the European Union. It has a role as a melanin synthesis inhibitor and an antifungal agrochemical. According to CHEBI, this compound is not a member of the azole group of antifungals, but is instead part of a group called the triazobenzothiazoles.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:24084639</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009184 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009184">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009317"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Mebendazole has a role as an antinematodal drug, a tubulin modulator, and a fungal microtubule-destabilising agent.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>CAS:31431-39-7</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>ChEMBL:CHEMBL685</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PubChem:4030</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009184</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>mebendazole</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009184"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Mebendazole has a role as an antinematodal drug, a tubulin modulator, and a fungal microtubule-destabilising agent.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:35149760</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009185 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009185">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009305"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Azoxystrobin (brand name Amistar, Syngenta) is widely used as a very broad spectrum fungicide in agriculture, particularly in wheat farming. It is an inhibitor of mitochondrial respiration by blocking electron transfer between cytochromes b and c1, and binds very tightly to the Qo site of Complex III of the mitochondrial electron transport chain, thereby ultimately preventing the generation of ATP.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>CAS:131860-33-8</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>CHEBI:40909</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>ChEMBL:CHEMBL230001</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PubChem:3034285</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009185</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>azoxystrobin</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009185"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Azoxystrobin (brand name Amistar, Syngenta) is widely used as a very broad spectrum fungicide in agriculture, particularly in wheat farming. It is an inhibitor of mitochondrial respiration by blocking electron transfer between cytochromes b and c1, and binds very tightly to the Qo site of Complex III of the mitochondrial electron transport chain, thereby ultimately preventing the generation of ATP.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:8206299</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:9850581</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009186 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009186">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009539"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Boscalid is a broad spectrum fungicide that is a succinate dehydrogenase (specifically complex II in the mitochondria) inhibitor. It ultimately blocks electron transport and ATP production in the mitochondrial electron transport chain, disrupting fungal respiration and leads to cell death or halted growth.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>CAS:188425-85-6</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>CHEBI:81822</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>ChEMBL:CHEMBL1076544</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PubChem:213013</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009186</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>boscalid</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009186"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Boscalid is a broad spectrum fungicide that is a succinate dehydrogenase (specifically complex II in the mitochondria) inhibitor. It ultimately blocks electron transport and ATP production in the mitochondrial electron transport chain, disrupting fungal respiration and leads to cell death or halted growth.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:21469935</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:24481672</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:30731698</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:35430062</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009187 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009187">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3007499"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Bromuconazole is a member of the class of oxolanes and is a foliar applied conazole fungicide for a range of crops. It is a dichlorobenzene, an organobromine compound, a member of oxolanes, a member of triazoles, a conazole fungicide and a triazole fungicide.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>CAS:116255-48-2</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>CHEBI:81900</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>ChEMBL:CHEMBL271579</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PubChem:3444</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009187</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>bromuconazole</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009187"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Bromuconazole is a member of the class of oxolanes and is a foliar applied conazole fungicide for a range of crops. It is a dichlorobenzene, an organobromine compound, a member of oxolanes, a member of triazoles, a conazole fungicide and a triazole fungicide.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:15146917</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009188 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009188">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009165"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Cycloheximide appears as colorless crystals that can be used as a fungicide and as a anticancer drug. It is produced by the bacterium Streptomyces griseus and has a role as a bacterial metabolite, a protein synthesis inhibitor, a neuroprotective agent, an anticoronaviral agent and a ferroptosis inhibitor. It is a member of piperidones, a piperidine antibiotic, a fungicide, a dicarboximide, a secondary alcohol and a cyclic ketone. It is functionally related to a piperidine-2,6-dione. Cycloheximide can cause developmental toxicity.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>CAS:4630-75-5</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>CHEBI:27641</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>ChEMBL:CHEMBL123292</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PubChem:2900</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009188</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>cycloheximide</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009188"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Cycloheximide appears as colorless crystals that can be used as a fungicide and as a anticancer drug. It is produced by the bacterium Streptomyces griseus and has a role as a bacterial metabolite, a protein synthesis inhibitor, a neuroprotective agent, an anticoronaviral agent and a ferroptosis inhibitor. It is a member of piperidones, a piperidine antibiotic, a fungicide, a dicarboximide, a secondary alcohol and a cyclic ketone. It is functionally related to a piperidine-2,6-dione. Cycloheximide can cause developmental toxicity.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:9271421</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009189 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009189">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3007499"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Cyproconazole is a fungicide that prevents wood decay from fungi in above-ground applications, but is not intended to protect trees in the ground.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>CAS:94361-06-5</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>CHEBI:83748</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>ChEMBL:CHEMBL2131954</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PubChem:86132</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009189</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>cyproconazole</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009189"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Cyproconazole is a fungicide that prevents wood decay from fungi in above-ground applications, but is not intended to protect trees in the ground.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:23589453</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009190 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009190">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3007499"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Difenoconazole is a broad spectrum fungicide with efficacy against some members of the Aschomycetes, Basidomycetes and Deuteromycetes families. It acts as a seed treatment, foliar spray and systemic fungicide. It is taken up through the surface of the infected plant and is translocated to all parts of the plant. It has a curative effect and a preventative effect. The mode of action of difenoconazole is that it is a sterol demethylation inhibitor which prevents the development of the fungus by inhibiting cell membrane ergosterol biosynthesis.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>CAS:119446-68-3</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>CHEBI:81760</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>ChEMBL:CHEMBL466426</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PubChem:86173</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009190</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>difenoconazole</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009190"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Difenoconazole is a broad spectrum fungicide with efficacy against some members of the Aschomycetes, Basidomycetes and Deuteromycetes families. It acts as a seed treatment, foliar spray and systemic fungicide. It is taken up through the surface of the infected plant and is translocated to all parts of the plant. It has a curative effect and a preventative effect. The mode of action of difenoconazole is that it is a sterol demethylation inhibitor which prevents the development of the fungus by inhibiting cell membrane ergosterol biosynthesis.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:26863444</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009191 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009191">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3007499"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Epoxiconazole was introduced in 1990 as a broad-spectrum fungicide with long-lasting activity. Even though epoxiconazole is produced and commercialized as a racemic compound, not surprisingly, a recent publication indicates that the optically pure (2S,3R) enantiomer of epoxiconazole is much more active against pathogenic fungi than its (2R,3S)-enantiomer, whereas the plant growth regulatory activity mainly rests in the (2R,3S)-enantiomer.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>CAS:133855-98-8</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PubChem:107901</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009191</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>epoxiconazole</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009191"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Epoxiconazole was introduced in 1990 as a broad-spectrum fungicide with long-lasting activity. Even though epoxiconazole is produced and commercialized as a racemic compound, not surprisingly, a recent publication indicates that the optically pure (2S,3R) enantiomer of epoxiconazole is much more active against pathogenic fungi than its (2R,3S)-enantiomer, whereas the plant growth regulatory activity mainly rests in the (2R,3S)-enantiomer.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:29507067</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009192 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009192">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3002388"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>OXY-1-7 is a OXY family class A extended-spectrum beta-lactamase identified from Klebsiella oxytoca.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>OXY-1-7</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009192</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>OXY-1-7</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009192"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>OXY-1-7</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/antibiotic_resistance#CARD_Short_Name"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009193 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009193">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3000045"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Ontological connectedness between entities and/or interactions representing their relatedness or influence (paraphrased from Systems Biology Ontology) specific to CARD&apos;s Antibiotic Resistance Ontology.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009193</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>ARO relationship type</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009194 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009194">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3007522"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_2000001"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3007497"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Fungal cytochrome P450 enzymes which include mutations associated with resistance to polyene antibiotics, including amphotericin.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009194</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>polyene-resistant fungal cytochrome P450 enzyme</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009195 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009195">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3000708"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Ergosterol is an essential cell membrane mycosterol unique to fungi and some protozoa, and functionally analogous to cholesterol in animals. The ergosterol synthesis pathway in fungi is a common target for antifungal drugs, where ergosterol is either disrupted directly (as with amphotericin) or ergosterol synthesis is prevented at an earlier step (as with azole antibiotics inhibiting demethylation of lanosterol). In either case this results in cell death. Mutations in this enzymes may inhibit antifungal action, thereby conferring resistance to these compounds.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009195</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>fungal ergosterol synthesis pathway enzyme</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009196 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009196">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3000708"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Glucosyltransferase enzymes found in fungal organisms which are known targets for antifungal drugs, such as beta-1,3-glucan synthase. Antifungal medications such as echinocandins and caspofungin selectively target and inhibit these enzymes, preventing the formation of vital oligosaccharides and polysaccharides and thereby resulting in cell death. Mutations in these enzymes may prohibit antifungal binding and activity, thus associated with antifungal resistance.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009196</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>fungal cell wall synthesis enzyme</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009197 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009197">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3000708"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A family of enzymes involved in pyrimidine metabolism, including uracil phosphoribosyltransferases and uridylate kinases, among others. Certain antifungal drugs target these enzymes to disrupt this pathway, for example by preventing the formation of UMP, thereby hindering cellular metabolism and resulting in cell death. Mutations in these enzymes may prevent antifungal activity and are thus associated with antifungal resistance.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009197</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>fungal pyrimidine metabolism pathway enzyme targeted by antibiotic</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009198 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009198">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009621"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_2000000"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3007514"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0001000"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009313"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Mitochondrial electron transport chain (ETC) proteins involved in the respiratory chain complex for aerobic respiration in fungal organisms. Mutations in these proteins are associated with antifungal resistance by disrupting the inhibitory effect of certain drugs, such as Azoxystrobin.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009198</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>Candida albicans Erg11 with mutations conferring resistance to isavuconazole</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009199 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009199">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3000708"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Mitochrondrial electron transport chains are essential for cellular aerobic respiration in fungal organisms. These are a series of protein complexes which produce energy through the exergonic transfer of electrons from electron donors to electron receptors via redox reactions. They are associated with the citric acid cycle, fatty acid metabolism, and amino acid metabolism through aerobic respiration. Certain antibiotics, such as Azoxystrobin, target these proteins to block these reaction pathways to inhibit the formation of ATP, thus resulting in cell death. Mutations in these proteins are associated with antibiotic resistance by preventing the blocking mechanism of these antifungal drugs.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009199</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>fungal mitochondrial respiratory chain protein</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009200 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009200">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3007522"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_2000001"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3007499"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_2000001"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3007500"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_2000001"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009317"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The P450 enzyme system, which may be alternatively named the Cyp51 enzyme system in some fungi, is integral to ergosterol synthesis. Azoles target lanosterol demethylase thereby halting ergosterol production and ultimately compromises fungal cell membrane integrity. Mutations to the P450/Cyp51 genes have been shown to inhibit azole activity.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009200</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>azole-resistant fungal cytochrome P450 enzyme</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009201 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009201">
