        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009554</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>imazalil</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009554"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Imazalil is a member of the class of imidazoles.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:29507067</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009555 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009555">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009200"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_2000000"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009554"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0001000"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009401"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The fungal Cyp51 enzyme system belongs to the broader P450 family. It is integral to ergosterol synthesis. Azoles target lanosterol demethylase thereby halting ergosterol production and ultimately compromises fungal cell membrane integrity. Mutations to Cyp51 genes have been shown to inhibit azole activity.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009555</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>Aspergillus flavus Cyp51C with mutations conferring resistance to imazalil</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009556 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009556">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009200"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_2000000"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009553"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0001000"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009401"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The fungal Cyp51 enzyme system belongs to the broader P450 family. It is integral to ergosterol synthesis. Azoles target lanosterol demethylase thereby halting ergosterol production and ultimately compromises fungal cell membrane integrity. Mutations to Cyp51 genes have been shown to inhibit azole activity.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009556</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>Aspergillus flavus Cyp51C with mutations conferring resistance to myclobutanil</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009557 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009557">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009246"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_2000000"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3007501"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0001000"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009401"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Echinocandins inhibit beta-1,3-D-glucan synthase, disrupting fungal cell wall biosynthesis rather than sterol production. Although resistance to echinocandins is classically driven by mutations in the FKS genes, alterations in sterol biosynthesis pathways, including mutations in Cyp51, have been shown in some fungi to indirectly influence echinocandin susceptibility. Cyp51 mutations can modify membrane sterol composition and cell envelope architecture, affecting cell wall stress responses and compensatory signaling pathways. These secondary effects may reduce echinocandin efficacy and contribute to tolerance or reduced susceptibility, even though Cyp51 is not the direct molecular target of echinocandins.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009557</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>Aspergillus fumigatus Cyp51A with mutations conferring resistance to caspofungin</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009558 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009558">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009363"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>HapE codes for a transcription factor typically associated with cellular adaptation and is found within fungi.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009558</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>antifungal susceptible HapE</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009559 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009559">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009413"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_2000000"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3007508"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0001000"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009314"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>In Candida albicans, FKS1 encodes a catalytic subunit of the beta-1,3-D-glucan synthase complex typically associated with resistance to echinocandin antifungals. Although azoles primarily target ergosterol biosynthesis, rare FKS1 mutations have been reported to confer resistance. These mutations are thought to alter cell wall integrity and membrane to cell wall interactions, triggering compensatory stress response pathways that indirectly mitigate azole-mediated growth inhibition. Such cases represent uncommon, context-dependent resistance mechanisms rather than direct target-site resistance.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009559</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>Candida albicans FKS1 beta-1,3-glucan with mutations conferring resistance to fluconazole</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009559"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>In Candida albicans, FKS1 encodes a catalytic subunit of the beta-1,3-D-glucan synthase complex typically associated with resistance to echinocandin antifungals. Although azoles primarily target ergosterol biosynthesis, rare FKS1 mutations have been reported to confer resistance. These mutations are thought to alter cell wall integrity and membrane to cell wall interactions, triggering compensatory stress response pathways that indirectly mitigate azole-mediated growth inhibition. Such cases represent uncommon, context-dependent resistance mechanisms rather than direct target-site resistance.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:40790106</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009560 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009560">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3007546"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_2000000"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3007503"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0001000"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009314"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Echinocandin antifungals inhibits beta-1,3-D-glucan synthase, an enzyme complex essential for fungal cell wall biosynthesis. Mutations in FKS genes compromise this enzyme and can confer resistance to echinocandin drug compounds.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009560</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>Candida dubliniensis FKS1 beta-1,3-glucan synthase with mutations conferring resistance to anidulafungin</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009561 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009561">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009562"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>ATIC is a bifunctional enzyme that catalyzes the final two steps of the de novo purine biosynthesis pathway in most eukaryotes. Its gene, ADE17, produces a single polypeptide chain that folds into two distinct domains. The first domain isthe 5-aminoimidazole-4-carboxamide ribonucleotide (AICAR) Transformylase domain which takes the substrate AICAR and a folate molecule to add a carbon atom. The second is the IMP Cyclohydrolase domain which takes the result of the first reaction and transforms it into a ring to create Inosine Monophosphate (IMP), which is the precursor to all DNA/RNA bases. AICAR transformylase refers to the specific functional domain of the ADE17 enzyme that is targeted or inhibited by certain chemical compounds. The ATIC enzyme is not typically associated with echinocandin resistance in a mutational context. When a fungi experiences stress of its cell wall (like echinocandin induced stress) it responds by up regulating gene transcription to repair the wall. ADE17 is one of these genes.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009561</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>echinocandin resistant fungal ATIC enzyme</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009562 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009562">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_0000031"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>ATIC is a bifunctional enzyme that catalyzes the final two steps of the de novo purine biosynthesis pathway in most eukaryotes. Its gene, ADE17, produces a single polypeptide chain that folds into two distinct domains. The first domain isthe 5-aminoimidazole-4-carboxamide ribonucleotide (AICAR) Transformylase domain which takes the substrate AICAR and a folate molecule to add a carbon atom. The second is the IMP Cyclohydrolase domain which takes the result of the first reaction and transforms it into a ring to create Inosine Monophosphate (IMP), which is the precursor to all DNA/RNA bases. AICAR transformylase refers to the specific functional domain of the ADE17 enzyme that is targeted or inhibited by certain chemical compounds.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009562</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>antifungal resistant ATIC enzyme</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009563 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009563">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009240"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>In fungi, the Tac1 gene is a zinc cluster transcription factor that influences the transcription of several proteins related to antifungal resistance. Of particular note, Tac1 influences the expression of CDR1 and CDR2 which are multi-drug efflux transporters.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009563</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>allylamine resistant fungal TAC1</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009564 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009564">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009565"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_2000001"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3000003"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>BycA specifically influences the morphological state of fungi by unrestricting Protein Kinase A (PKA). Under normal conditions calcineurin acts as the master regulator by suppressing the transcription of the bycA gene at the mRNA level. By keeping BycA levels minimized, downstream PKA activity remains low which ultimately permits virulent hyphal growth in fungi. When calcineurin is inhibited the transcriptional suppression on the bycA promoter is immediately lifted. This ultimately causes the fungal cell to bind extracellular amino acids leading to a conformational change that forces the fungus into a yeast-locked, avirulent state.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009564</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>calcineurin inhibitor resistant BycA</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009565 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009565">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3003768"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_2000001"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3000003"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Transcribed BycA is a protein that influences the morphological state of fungi by unrestricting Protein Kinase A (PKA). Under normal conditions calcineurin acts as the master regulator by suppressing the transcription of the bycA gene at the mRNA level. By keeping BycA levels minimized, downstream PKA activity remains low which ultimately permits virulent hyphal growth in fungi. When calcineurin is inhibited the transcriptional suppression on the bycA promoter is immediately lifted. This ultimately causes the fungal cell to bind extracellular amino acids leading to a conformational change that forces the fungus into a yeast-locked, avirulent state.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009565</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>fungal BycA</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009566 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009566">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3007683"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>In fungi, unregulated protein kinase A can cause a morphological transition change from an avirulent yeast form to a virulent, filamentous hyphae form.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009566</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>fungal Protein Kinase A</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009567 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009567">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3000045"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Type of variation reported by resistance gene annotators or interpreters. Based on the schemes developed by hAMRonization (https://github.com/pha4ge/hAMRonization) and AMRRules (https://docs.amrrules.org), with additional terms from the NCIT ontology.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>antibiotic_resistance</oboInOwl:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboInOwl:id>ARO:3009567</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:label>ARO variant type</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009568 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ARO_3009568">
